      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Published by Elsevier Inc.",
        "AbstractText": "Opioid-induced sleep disordered breathing presents a therapeutic predicament with the increasing incidence of prescription opioid use for noncancer chronic pain in the United States. Central sleep apnea with a Biot or cluster breathing pattern is characteristic of polysomnography studies; however, long-term clinical outcomes and the impact of therapy remain unknown. Novel ampakine-based therapies are being investigated. Randomized controlled trials with therapies that target the underlying pathophysiologic mechanisms of opioid-induced sleep disordered breathing are required."
      },
